## synchronized vs ReentrantLock

```java
// synchronized — simple, automatic unlock
public class Counter {
    private int count = 0;

    public synchronized void increment() { count++; }   // Lock on 'this'
    public synchronized int getCount() { return count; }

    // Block-level synchronization:
    public void conditionalIncrement(int max) {
        synchronized (this) {
            if (count < max) count++;
        }  // Unlocks automatically (even on exception)
    }
}

// ReentrantLock — more control
public class BankAccount {
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock(true);  // Fair lock

    public boolean withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
        try {
            if (!lock.tryLock(100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
                return false;  // Non-blocking — don't wait forever
            }
            try {
                if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0) return false;
                balance = balance.subtract(amount);
                return true;
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();  // Must unlock manually
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

## Atomic Classes — Lock-Free Operations

```java
public class RequestCounter {
    private final AtomicLong requestCount = new AtomicLong(0);
    private final AtomicLong errorCount = new AtomicLong(0);

    public void recordRequest(boolean error) {
        requestCount.incrementAndGet();  // Thread-safe, no lock
        if (error) errorCount.incrementAndGet();
    }

    public long getRequestCount() { return requestCount.get(); }
    public double getErrorRate() {
        long requests = requestCount.get();
        return requests == 0 ? 0 : (double) errorCount.get() / requests;
    }
}

// AtomicReference — thread-safe reference updates
AtomicReference<Config> configRef = new AtomicReference<>(initialConfig);

// CAS: compare current to expected, update only if they match
boolean updated = configRef.compareAndSet(expectedOldConfig, newConfig);
// Returns false if another thread already updated it
```

## Concurrent Collections

```java
// ConcurrentHashMap — segment-level locking (Java 8+: CAS + synchronized per bucket)
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
map.put("key", 1);  // Thread-safe
map.merge("key", 1, Integer::sum);  // Atomic increment
map.compute("key", (k, v) -> v == null ? 1 : v + 1);  // Atomic compute

// CopyOnWriteArrayList — read-heavy, rarely modified
CopyOnWriteArrayList<String> list = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
list.add("item");  // Creates new copy of array — safe to iterate simultaneously
// Iterators don't throw ConcurrentModificationException
// BAD for high write frequency — copies entire array per write

// BlockingQueue — producer-consumer
BlockingQueue<Task> taskQueue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(100);

// Producer:
taskQueue.put(task);     // Blocks if full
taskQueue.offer(task);   // Non-blocking, returns false if full
taskQueue.offer(task, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);  // Waits up to 5 sec

// Consumer:
Task task = taskQueue.take();    // Blocks until item available
Task task2 = taskQueue.poll(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);  // Waits up to 5 sec, null if empty
```

## ThreadLocal — Per-Thread State

```java
// Each thread has its own value — no sharing, no synchronization
ThreadLocal<SimpleDateFormat> dateFormat = ThreadLocal.withInitial(
    () -> new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd")  // SimpleDateFormat is not thread-safe
);

// In method — safe because each thread has its own formatter:
public String formatDate(Date date) {
    return dateFormat.get().format(date);  // Thread-local instance
}

// IMPORTANT: Remove ThreadLocal in thread pool contexts!
// Thread pool reuses threads — if you don't clean up, next task sees stale data:
dateFormat.remove();  // Call in finally block or filter cleanup

// Spring's RequestContextHolder uses ThreadLocal:
public String getCurrentUserId() {
    return (String) RequestContextHolder.getRequestAttributes()
        .getAttribute("userId", RequestAttributes.SCOPE_REQUEST);
}
```

## Production Reality

In Spring applications, most thread safety concerns are handled by the framework: Spring beans are typically stateless singletons — no shared mutable state. The problems arise when you add static or instance fields to Spring beans. If you find yourself adding `private SomeState state;` to a `@Service`, that's a thread safety concern waiting to happen. Favor stateless services, and when state is needed, use `ThreadLocal`, `ConcurrentHashMap`, or push state to the database.

## ThreadPoolExecutor Configuration

```java
// Explicit configuration — don't use Executors factory for production:
ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
    10,                              // corePoolSize
    50,                              // maximumPoolSize
    60L, TimeUnit.SECONDS,           // keepAliveTime (idle thread cleanup)
    new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1000),  // workQueue — bounded! prevents OOM
    new ThreadFactory() {
        private final AtomicInteger threadNum = new AtomicInteger();
        @Override
        public Thread newThread(Runnable r) {
            Thread t = new Thread(r, "order-processor-" + threadNum.incrementAndGet());
            t.setDaemon(false);  // User threads — JVM waits for them
            return t;
        }
    },
    new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy()  // Rejection policy
    // CallerRunsPolicy: when queue full, calling thread executes the task
    // — applies backpressure instead of dropping tasks
);

// Rejection policies:
// AbortPolicy (default): throws RejectedExecutionException
// CallerRunsPolicy: calling thread executes the task (backpressure)
// DiscardPolicy: silently drops the task
// DiscardOldestPolicy: drops oldest queued task, retries new one
```

## Spring @Async with Custom Thread Pool

```java
@Configuration
@EnableAsync
public class AsyncConfig {

    @Bean("emailTaskExecutor")
    public Executor emailTaskExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(5);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(20);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(100);
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("email-async-");
        executor.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());
        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }

    @Bean("reportTaskExecutor")
    public Executor reportTaskExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(2);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(5);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(50);
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("report-async-");
        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }
}

// Use specific executor:
@Service
public class NotificationService {
    @Async("emailTaskExecutor")  // Uses the named executor
    public CompletableFuture<Void> sendEmailAsync(String to, String body) {
        emailClient.send(to, body);  // Runs in email thread pool
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(null);
    }
}
```

## CompletableFuture with Executor

```java
Executor ioExecutor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(20);  // IO-bound tasks
Executor cpuExecutor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(
    Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors()  // CPU-bound
);

// Parallel data fetch:
CompletableFuture<User> userFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
    () -> userService.findById(userId), ioExecutor
);

CompletableFuture<List<Order>> ordersFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
    () -> orderRepo.findByUserId(userId), ioExecutor
);

// Combine results when both complete:
CompletableFuture<DashboardData> dashboardFuture =
    userFuture.thenCombine(ordersFuture,
        (user, orders) -> new DashboardData(user, orders)
    );

DashboardData dashboard = dashboardFuture.join();  // Wait for result
```

## Thread Pool Sizing Guidelines

```
IO-bound tasks (DB queries, HTTP calls, file IO):
  Threads spend ~90% waiting → can have many
  Rule: (# cores) × (1 + wait_time / compute_time)
  E.g.: 4 cores, 100ms wait / 10ms compute → 4 × (1 + 10) = 44 threads
  Practical: start with 20-50, adjust based on monitoring

CPU-bound tasks (compression, encryption, image processing):
  Threads spend most time computing → don't exceed core count
  Rule: # cores (or # cores + 1 for scheduling)
  E.g.: 8 core machine → 8-9 threads for CPU work
  More threads: context switching overhead

Mixed workloads: separate pools for IO and CPU tasks
```

## Shutdown and Cleanup

```java
// Proper shutdown:
executor.shutdown();  // No new tasks, finish existing
try {
    if (!executor.awaitTermination(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
        executor.shutdownNow();   // Cancel in-progress tasks
        if (!executor.awaitTermination(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
            System.err.println("Thread pool did not terminate");
        }
    }
} catch (InterruptedException e) {
    executor.shutdownNow();
    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
}

// Spring's TaskExecutor: lifecycle managed automatically
// Beans with @PreDestroy / DisposableBean shutdown cleanly
```

## Production Reality

Expose thread pool metrics via Micrometer: active threads, queued tasks, completed tasks, and rejection count. Set alerting on rejection count — rejections mean you're dropping work or applying backpressure. Size pools based on measured performance under load, not theory — run load tests and watch thread pool metrics in Grafana.

## volatile — Visibility Without Atomicity

```java
// Problem without volatile:
public class StopThread {
    private static boolean stopRequested = false;  // NOT volatile

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread thread = new Thread(() -> {
            while (!stopRequested) {  // Thread may cache this in register!
                // JVM can optimize: if (!stopRequested) while(true) { }
                // Thread never sees the update from main thread!
            }
            System.out.println("Stopped");
        });
        thread.start();

        Thread.sleep(1000);
        stopRequested = true;  // Main thread writes — worker may never see it
        // Result: infinite loop on some JVMs (especially with -server flag)
    }
}

// Fix with volatile:
private static volatile boolean stopRequested = false;
// Now: write in main thread immediately visible to worker thread

// volatile compound operation — NOT atomic:
private volatile int count = 0;

public void increment() {
    count++;  // 3 ops: read count, add 1, write count
    // Thread A: reads count=5
    // Thread B: reads count=5
    // Thread A: writes count=6
    // Thread B: writes count=6  ← Lost update!
}

// Fix: AtomicInteger
private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);
public void increment() { count.incrementAndGet(); }  // Atomic CAS
```

## Happens-Before Rules

```java
// Rule 1: Program order — within a single thread, each statement
// happens-before the next
int x = 1;   // HB
int y = x;   // HB — guaranteed to see x=1

// Rule 2: Monitor lock — unlock happens-before subsequent lock
synchronized (lock) { a = 1; }   // Thread A
synchronized (lock) { int b = a; } // Thread B — guaranteed b=1

// Rule 3: volatile — write happens-before subsequent read
volatile int flag = 0;
flag = 1;       // Thread A
if (flag == 1)  // Thread B — guaranteed to see 1

// Rule 4: Thread.start() — parent happens-before child starts
shared = 42;    // Main thread
t.start();      // Child thread sees shared=42

// Rule 5: Thread.join() — child happens-before parent resumes
t.start();
/* t does: result = computeAnswer(); */
t.join();       // Main thread waits
use(result);    // Guaranteed to see result set by t

// Rule 6: Transitivity — if A HB B and B HB C, then A HB C
```

## Double-Checked Locking — volatile Required

```java
// BROKEN without volatile:
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton instance;  // NOT volatile!

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {                    // Check 1
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {            // Check 2
                    instance = new Singleton();    // Problem!
                    // Object construction and reference write can be REORDERED:
                    // 1. Allocate memory
                    // 2. Write reference to instance (NOT YET CONSTRUCTED)
                    // 3. Call constructor
                    // Another thread sees non-null but partially constructed object!
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}

// CORRECT with volatile:
private static volatile Singleton instance;
// volatile prevents reordering of object construction
// Write to instance happens AFTER full construction

// Even better: initialization-on-demand holder (no volatile needed):
public class Singleton {
    private Singleton() {}

    private static class Holder {
        static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();
        // Class loading is thread-safe — initialized exactly once
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        return Holder.INSTANCE;  // No synchronization needed
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

In modern Spring applications, you rarely need to write low-level concurrency code with `volatile` directly — `AtomicInteger`, `ConcurrentHashMap`, and `CompletableFuture` handle most cases. Understanding `volatile` and happens-before matters for: (1) code reviews catching unsafe publications, (2) understanding why `HashMap` in a Spring singleton bean can be corrupted under concurrent access, (3) understanding why `@Scope("singleton")` beans with mutable state are dangerous.

## Classic Deadlock

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private final long id;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    // DEADLOCK RISK:
    public static void transfer(BankAccount from, BankAccount to, BigDecimal amount) {
        synchronized (from.lock) {      // Thread A: locks account 1
            synchronized (to.lock) {    // Thread A: tries to lock account 2
                from.balance = from.balance.subtract(amount);
                to.balance = to.balance.add(amount);
            }
        }
        // Meanwhile:
        // Thread B: transfer(account2, account1, ...) locks account 2, tries account 1
        // → Circular wait → Deadlock!
    }
}
```

## Fix 1: Consistent Lock Ordering

```java
// Always lock lower ID account first — breaks circular wait
public static void transfer(BankAccount from, BankAccount to, BigDecimal amount) {
    BankAccount first  = from.id < to.id ? from : to;
    BankAccount second = from.id < to.id ? to : from;

    synchronized (first.lock) {    // Always lower ID first
        synchronized (second.lock) {
            from.balance = from.balance.subtract(amount);
            to.balance = to.balance.add(amount);
        }
    }
    // Thread A: lock account1 (id=1), lock account2 (id=2)
    // Thread B: lock account1 (id=1) → waits → no circular wait!
}
```

## Fix 2: tryLock() with Timeout

```java
public static boolean transfer(BankAccount from, BankAccount to, BigDecimal amount)
        throws InterruptedException {
    while (true) {
        if (from.lock.tryLock(100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
            try {
                if (to.lock.tryLock(100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
                    try {
                        from.balance = from.balance.subtract(amount);
                        to.balance = to.balance.add(amount);
                        return true;
                    } finally {
                        to.lock.unlock();
                    }
                }
            } finally {
                from.lock.unlock();
            }
        }
        // Couldn't acquire both locks — back off and retry
        Thread.sleep(ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong(10, 50));  // Random backoff
    }
}
// If both threads can't get locks → both release → retry → eventually one succeeds
```

## Deadlock Detection

```java
// Programmatic detection:
ThreadMXBean mxBean = ManagementFactory.getThreadMXBean();
long[] deadlockedThreadIds = mxBean.findDeadlockedThreads();
if (deadlockedThreadIds != null) {
    ThreadInfo[] threadInfos = mxBean.getThreadInfo(deadlockedThreadIds, true, true);
    for (ThreadInfo threadInfo : threadInfos) {
        System.out.println("Deadlocked thread: " + threadInfo.getThreadName());
        System.out.println("Waiting for lock: " + threadInfo.getLockName());
        System.out.println("Held by: " + threadInfo.getLockOwnerName());
    }
    // Send alert, trigger thread dump, etc.
}

// Scheduled check:
@Scheduled(fixedDelay = 60000)
public void checkForDeadlocks() {
    long[] deadlocked = mxBean.findDeadlockedThreads();
    if (deadlocked != null) {
        alertService.sendAlert("DEADLOCK DETECTED", deadlocked.length + " threads deadlocked");
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

Deadlocks in Spring applications often originate from: (1) `@Transactional` locking rows in different orders across services, (2) using `synchronized` in Spring singletons with complex call paths, (3) database-level deadlocks from `SELECT FOR UPDATE` in different transaction order. For DB-level deadlocks, the database detects and rolls back one transaction — ensure you handle `CannotAcquireLockException` / `DeadlockLoserDataAccessException` with retry logic using `@Retryable`.
